                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a1352f15-572c-3f93-a004-6572c3a56e76",
                        "entry_id": "6f9c46db-9b6e-30ad-95ab-c066bdf18ab1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To peer over; to overlook."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "15f883ec-e60e-4861-babc-c7f5f1ba1a99",
                                "sense_id": "a1352f15-572c-3f93-a004-6572c3a56e76",
                                "text": "[…] the English, in the suburbs close intrench’d,\nWont, through a secret grate of iron bars\nIn yonder tower, to overpeer the city,\nAnd thence discover how with most advantage\nThey may vex us with shot, or with assault."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d089ad77-5c17-43f4-a9a0-66d684540484",
                                "sense_id": "a1352f15-572c-3f93-a004-6572c3a56e76",
                                "text": "1906, Arthur Quiller-Couch (under the pseudonym “Q”), The Mayor of Troy, London: Methuen, Chapter 1, p. 16,\nIn Admirals’ Row […] Miss Sally Tregentil would overpeer her blind and draw back in a flutter lest the Major had observed her."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "48bdef5c-d0f2-38d0-a52f-08fc66abaef0",
                        "entry_id": "6f9c46db-9b6e-30ad-95ab-c066bdf18ab1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To rise above."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "811b55a3-491c-4024-b23c-5e121db87934",
                                "sense_id": "48bdef5c-d0f2-38d0-a52f-08fc66abaef0",
                                "text": "The ocean, overpeering of his list,\nEats not the flats with more impetuous haste\nThan Young Laertes, in a riotous head,\nO’erbears Your offices."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7bb7e08-27e8-4377-9d5d-7bf909aaee77",
                                "sense_id": "48bdef5c-d0f2-38d0-a52f-08fc66abaef0",
                                "text": "1896, Charles G. D. Roberts, The Forge in the Forest, Boston: Lamson, Wolffe & Co., Part I, Foreward, p. 12,\nThese patches are but meagre second growth, with here and there a gnarled birch or overpeering pine, lonely survivor of the primeval brotherhood."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
